1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,470
Open Workflows in the Chicago
Fire demonstration department.

2
00:00:03,849 --> 00:00:07,809
We will build a training call-created
workflow with two ordered actions

3
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:09,960
and a separate call-closed workflow.

4
00:00:10,479 --> 00:00:13,529
Begin with the outcome you want
and the event that should cause it.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,170
Each workflow listens to one event.

6
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:19,469
Multiple steps can respond to
that event, while a different

7
00:00:19,469 --> 00:00:21,049
event needs a different workflow.

8
00:00:21,309 --> 00:00:24,950
Inspect the list's event and
enabled columns before editing,

9
00:00:25,359 --> 00:00:29,089
and identify who will monitor the
result when the automation runs.

10
00:00:29,309 --> 00:00:32,740
Open Credentials and create
Academy local captured mail.

11
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:35,229
Choose SMTP Email.

12
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:39,569
In this isolated exercise,
Mailpit receives messages inside

13
00:00:39,569 --> 00:00:43,099
the training network; nothing
goes to an outside mail service.

14
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:47,570
Set the local host, port and
demonstration sender, keep the

15
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:49,849
unused password masked, and save.

16
00:00:50,329 --> 00:00:53,509
Your live department needs its
own mail provider settings and

17
00:00:53,509 --> 00:00:55,220
appropriate transport security.

18
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:58,040
The credential type must match the action.

19
00:00:58,279 --> 00:01:01,960
A stored credential supplies
authentication details; it does

20
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:06,240
not establish that a destination
is reachable or accepts a message.

21
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:10,899
Choose New Workflow and name it
Academy call opened — captured mail.

22
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:13,649
Select CallAdded as Trigger Event.

23
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:17,660
Turn Enabled off while configuring
the destination and actions,

24
00:01:17,859 --> 00:01:19,590
and review the retry controls.

25
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:23,820
The event becomes fixed after
creation, so confirm it before saving.

26
00:01:24,219 --> 00:01:28,789
Keep retry values modest for practice
and observe actual attempt timing later.

27
00:01:28,909 --> 00:01:32,649
Template gallery examples also need
their destinations and credentials

28
00:01:32,649 --> 00:01:34,249
completed before enabling.

29
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:38,419
A descriptive name makes the event
and operational purpose easier to

30
00:01:38,419 --> 00:01:40,209
recognize in the workflow list.

31
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:41,749
Select Add Step.

32
00:01:42,209 --> 00:01:45,169
Choose Send Email and set Order to one.

33
00:01:45,669 --> 00:01:49,500
Enter the fictional training-supervisor
address and a subject, then

34
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:53,700
select Academy local captured
mail as the SMTP credential.

35
00:01:54,149 --> 00:01:58,459
In Output Template, use a short training
message with department name and call

36
00:01:58,459 --> 00:02:00,110
name from the variable reference.

37
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:04,360
Keep unnecessary personal details
and narratives out of the message.

38
00:02:04,519 --> 00:02:07,319
Save the step and inspect
its card in the editor.

39
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:12,130
The card shows the action and a template
excerpt; the execution history will

40
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:14,349
show whether a send actually completed.

41
00:02:14,589 --> 00:02:17,329
Add a second Send Email step at Order two.

42
00:02:17,869 --> 00:02:21,880
For this exercise, run it only when
the call name contains TRAINING.

43
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:27,440
Use Visual Builder for simple comparisons,
or Raw Expression with Scriban braces.

44
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:29,400
Here, call.name

45
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:31,209
piped to string.contains

46
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:34,080
TRAINING must render
true for the step to run.

47
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:37,660
Select Test Condition and
read the result carefully.

48
00:02:37,789 --> 00:02:41,910
A syntax-valid message means the
expression can be read; without

49
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:45,879
sample event data, it does not
establish whether this call will pass.

50
00:02:46,369 --> 00:02:50,360
Save the condition with the step,
then inspect the actual run later to

51
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,350
confirm whether it ran or skipped.

52
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:58,190
Create Academy call closed — captured mail
as a second workflow, choose CallClosed,

53
00:02:58,369 --> 00:03:00,190
and add its own closeout action.

54
00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:02,290
Keep it disabled until checked.

55
00:03:02,669 --> 00:03:05,759
This gives each event a
visible definition and history.

56
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,129
It does not make the first
workflow wait for the second event.

57
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:13,340
For other operations, choose the
actual available event, such as

58
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:17,250
a unit status change, completed
checklist or finalized record.

59
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:21,299
Check the event's timing and data
before choosing template variables.

60
00:03:21,679 --> 00:03:25,329
Ordered steps belong to the event
that started that specific run.

61
00:03:25,590 --> 00:03:30,000
Reopen Academy call opened — captured
mail and review the destination,

62
00:03:30,190 --> 00:03:32,679
credential, enabled steps and order.

63
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:36,349
Confirm the training receiver and
background processing are ready.

64
00:03:36,809 --> 00:03:38,480
Enable the workflow and save.

65
00:03:39,009 --> 00:03:42,739
Create one approved training call
whose name contains TRAINING.

66
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:45,929
That operational event
is the execution test.

67
00:03:46,109 --> 00:03:49,639
If the action is meant for email,
a file store or a messaging

68
00:03:49,639 --> 00:03:53,420
platform, use a controlled test
destination for that service.

69
00:03:53,819 --> 00:03:57,979
Do not infer a working integration
merely from the workflow's enabled

70
00:03:57,979 --> 00:04:00,269
badge or the absence of a form error.

71
00:04:00,519 --> 00:04:03,839
Open Runs for the workflow,
then open the training run.

72
00:04:04,349 --> 00:04:08,210
Read its status, attempt number,
start and completion times.

73
00:04:08,570 --> 00:04:12,570
Inspect each step's log and duration,
including the conditional step.

74
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:16,529
Expand Rendered Output when your
permissions allow it and compare the

75
00:04:16,559 --> 00:04:18,170
actual message with the template.

76
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:22,469
A skipped step should have an
understandable reason; a failed step

77
00:04:22,570 --> 00:04:24,439
needs its recorded error reviewed.

78
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:28,499
Compare the receiver's own controlled
receipt when you need proof of delivery,

79
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:32,440
because a workflow log alone may
describe only the action's handoff.

80
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:37,769
Open Health to review recent successful,
failed and skipped runs and performance.

81
00:04:38,109 --> 00:04:40,700
Use the different time windows
to see whether a single

82
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:42,230
failure is part of a pattern.

83
00:04:42,789 --> 00:04:46,339
Pending Runs shows work that is
waiting, running or retrying.

84
00:04:46,789 --> 00:04:51,529
Inspect a run before using Cancel, and
understand what Clear Pending will affect.

85
00:04:51,709 --> 00:04:56,129
These controls manage queued work;
they do not undo an action already

86
00:04:56,129 --> 00:04:57,839
accepted by another service.

87
00:04:58,190 --> 00:05:02,240
Keep the run identifier when
investigating, so the workflow, event and

88
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:04,450
destination evidence remain connected.

89
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:09,660
For a controlled retry exercise, pause
only the isolated training receiver

90
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,530
and trigger a fresh close event.

91
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:16,190
In this example the same run reaches
three attempts, then fails with

92
00:05:16,190 --> 00:05:18,119
Maximum retry attempts exceeded.

93
00:05:18,529 --> 00:05:21,080
Read the SMTP error in its step logs.

94
00:05:21,449 --> 00:05:24,689
Restoring the receiver does
not restart an exhausted run.

95
00:05:24,909 --> 00:05:28,620
A fresh training close event creates
a different run, which completes on

96
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:30,769
attempt one and arrives in the receiver.

97
00:05:31,249 --> 00:05:35,989
Retries happen automatically; this page
does not provide a general Retry button.

98
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:39,659
Earlier actions can run
again, so destinations must

99
00:05:39,659 --> 00:05:41,209
tolerate duplicate attempts.

100
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:45,740
In this build, the saved thirty-second
backoff did not match the observed

101
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:49,709
five- and ten-second gaps, so
confirm timing in the run history.

102
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:53,730
Finally, disable the training
workflows and keep their run history.

103
00:05:54,169 --> 00:05:58,110
These controls also fit community
care and mental health teams, search

104
00:05:58,110 --> 00:06:02,430
and rescue organizations, security
departments, wildland contractors

105
00:06:02,470 --> 00:06:04,190
and industrial response teams.

106
00:06:04,669 --> 00:06:08,520
Choose the operational event and the
minimum information your own process

107
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:13,760
needs; for example, route a site incident
to a supervisor or a completed readiness

108
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:15,340
check to a training coordinator.


